{"id": "L2-refund-spec", "pass_score": 0.67, "prompt": "Build this spec and take it all the way to a change that is ready to ship. I am not available for questions, so make reasonable decisions and write them down.\n\nStatus: ready-for-agent\n\n# Wallet transfer refunds\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nCustomers sometimes send a wallet transfer to the wrong person. Today nothing can undo a transfer, so support moves money back by hand with a new transfer, which is error-prone and can pay out twice.\n\n## Solution\n\nA transfer gets an identifier. Support can refund a transfer by that identifier: the money goes back from the recipient to the sender, a transfer is refunded at most once, and a refund is never for more than was sent.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As a sender, I want every transfer to have an identifier, so that a specific transfer can be refunded later.\n2. As a support agent, I want to refund a transfer by its identifier, so that the money goes back to the sender.\n3. As a sender, I want a refund to return the amount to my balance and take it from the recipient, so that both balances are as before the transfer.\n4. As a support agent, I want a second refund of the same transfer to be rejected, so that the sender is never paid twice.\n5. As a support agent, I want a refund for more than the transfer amount to be rejected, so that the wallet never pays out more than was sent.\n6. As a support agent, I want a refund of an unknown transfer to be rejected, so that a typo cannot move money.\n7. As a support agent, I want a rejected refund to leave every balance unchanged, so that a failed refund has no side effects.\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n- One seam: the wallet module's public functions. `transfer` returns the transfer identifier; a new `refund(transferId, amount?)` refunds the full amount when no amount is given.\n- A refund records the transfer as refunded before any balance changes, so a repeated call cannot pay twice.\n- Rejections throw an error with a stable code: `TRANSFER_NOT_FOUND`, `ALREADY_REFUNDED`, `REFUND_EXCEEDS_TRANSFER`.\n- **Security:** assets are the sender's and recipient's balances. Threats: a replayed refund (tampering), a refund above the transfer (elevation), an unknown or forged identifier (spoofing). Controls: at most one refund per transfer, amount at most the transfer amount, unknown identifiers rejected, no balance change on rejection.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- Test only through the wallet module's public functions (deposit, transfer, refund, balance); assert balances and errors, never internal state.\n- Good tests: full refund restores both balances; a second refund is rejected; an amount above the transfer is rejected; an unknown identifier is rejected; each rejection leaves balances unchanged.\n- Prior art: the existing transfer test in test/wallet.test.js.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Partial refunds of the remaining amount after a first partial refund.\n- Persistence: balances stay in memory.\n- Changing balances from floating point to integer minor units.\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- Balances are JavaScript numbers; the existing behaviour is kept.\n", "env": {"TEA_SDLC_WORKFLOW": "full"}}
